# Next.js App Router Standards

- Use Next.js 16.1.6 or later (keep dependencies current).
- Assume Next.js App Router lives in `src/app`. Default to React Server Components; add `"use client"` only when interactivity is required.

## Structure
- `src/app/` — routing only (route groups + segments)
- `src/components/` — shared UI
- `src/lib/` — auth, validation, utilities, server-only logic
- `src/styles/` — global styles, tokens
- `src/types/` — shared TypeScript types
- `src/hooks/` — shared React hooks
- `src/config/` — runtime-safe config (no secrets in client)

## Route Groups (do not affect URLs)
- `src/app/(marketing)/`, `(app)/`, `(auth)/`, `(internal)/`
- Private folders: `src/app/_lib`, `_components`, `_types` — never `page.tsx` inside underscore folders

## Next.js Primitives
- `next/link` for navigation, `next/image` for images when beneficial
- Metadata API when applicable
- Route files: `page.tsx`, `layout.tsx`, `loading.tsx`, `error.tsx`, `not-found.tsx`, `route.ts`

## Security
- Do not rely on obscurity. Enforce authorization server-side.
- Return 401/403 as appropriate. Validate and sanitize inputs on the server.
- Auth: prefer `src/app/(auth)/sign-in`, `sign-up`, `reset-password`
- API routes: `src/app/api/*/route.ts` with strict authorization
- Avoid `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` unless sanitized
